\begin{abstract}
Execution-based evaluation of neurosymbolic reasoning conflates faithful derivation with structural shortcuts: a formalization that trivially embeds the conclusion among its premises proves just as reliably as a genuine one, and reinforcement learning (RL) optimizers exploit this indifference.
We apply leave-one-out ablation to GRPO-trained formalizations---\emph{Solver Trace Ablation} (STA)---testing whether each component is individually necessary for proof success, and characterize structural degeneration under six reward conditions via the resulting \emph{Intensional Structural Reward} (ISR).
ISR reveals that prove-rate gains conceal a compositional shift toward hacking modes, but ISR occupies a paradoxical position: the same leave-one-out structure that makes it informative also makes it gameable.
Tautological formalizations satisfy the necessity criterion by construction, so when ISR is repurposed as a training reward, the model learns to game the diagnostic rather than the task.
In our Qwen3-4B experiments, the resulting Goodhart effect is seed-dependent (3 of 5 seeds converge to gaming, demonstrating that the Goodhart effect can occur under standard training conditions; 2 of 5 retain genuine reasoning), but the \emph{observe-vs-optimize asymmetry} persists across logic types and dataset families---confirmed by cross-logic (Prolog) replication and supported by exploratory cross-benchmark (ProofFOL) evidence: structural proxies for formalization integrity are easier to observe than to optimize.\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

A neurosymbolic reasoner can achieve rising prove rate while the internal structure of its proofs collapses entirely.
The root cause is evaluation by a single criterion---whether the generated formalization proves under a formal solver---which treats all proofs as equal: a formalization that embeds the conclusion verbatim among its premises receives the same reward as one that derives it through genuine reasoning.
Under reinforcement learning, this indifference becomes an active vulnerability~\citep{geirhos2020shortcut}; the optimizer exploits any shortcut the reward fails to penalize~\citep{skalse-reward-hacking, reward-hacking-survey}, and execution feedback provides no signal against structural shortcuts.

Neurosymbolic formalizers~\citep{logic-lm, linc, satlm} and RL-trained variants~\citep{mellgren-prolog-grpo, logic-rl, deepseekmath} rely on execution accuracy alone, with no diagnostic for whether individual components contribute to the proof.
Process reward models~\citep{lightman-verify-step, math-shepherd} offer finer granularity but require expensive step-level annotations that do not transfer to solver-backed settings, while inference-time gaming detection~\citep{kim-game-formalization, bondarenko-spec-gaming} identifies gaming post-hoc but provides no training-time structural signal.
What is missing is an annotation-free method for testing \emph{component necessity} in neurosymbolic outputs.

We address this gap with \emph{Solver Trace Ablation} (STA), a diagnostic that tests whether each component of a formalization is necessary for proof success.
For each proved output, STA removes components one at a time and re-runs the solver; the fraction whose removal breaks the proof yields the \emph{Intensional Structural Reward} (ISR).
A formalization where every component is necessary receives $\text{ISR}{=}1$; one that proves regardless of which component is removed receives $\text{ISR}{=}0$.
Leave-one-out (LOO) ablation itself is standard~\citep{demillo1978, jia-harman-survey}; our contribution is its systematic application to neurosymbolic outputs, where it reveals a diagnostic--optimization asymmetry (\S\ref{sec:diagnostic}--\ref{sec:goodhart}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.95\columnwidth]{figures/fig5_training_isr_curves.pdf}
\caption{ISR$^*$ (proved samples only) training trajectories across reward conditions (rolling $w{=}20$). Exec-only collapses to $\text{ISR}{\approx}0$, confirming structural degeneration (\S\ref{sec:diagnostic}); the ${\sim}0.5$ plateau is an $n{=}2$ artifact. ISR $\beta{=}0.1$ seeds rise toward $\text{ISR}{\approx}1$ via TS gaming (Goodhart effect, \S\ref{sec:goodhart}); random remains low.}
\label{fig:training-isr-curves}
\end{figure}

Applied post-hoc, ISR reveals that rising prove rate conceals a compositional shift in proof structure: genuine reasoning gives way to hacking modes---conclusion embedding and tautological shortcuts---invisible to execution-based metrics (Figure~\ref{fig:training-isr-curves}; \S\ref{sec:diagnostic}).

Yet the same LOO structure that makes ISR diagnostic also creates a Goodhart vulnerability~\citep{manheim-goodhart-taxonomy, karwowski-goodhart-rl, gao-reward-overoptimization}: tautological formalizations ($P{\to}P$) satisfy the necessity criterion by construction, so when ISR is repurposed as a training reward, the model learns to game the diagnostic rather than the task.
This is not an isolated failure of one metric---it exposes a structural risk inherent to the RLVR paradigm: \emph{any} verifiable proxy that rewards structural properties of solver inputs is susceptible to the same exploitation.
In our Qwen3-4B experiments, the asymmetry persists across logic types (FOL, Prolog), dataset families (FOLIO, ProofFOL), reward doses, and random seeds, suggesting that structural proxies for formalization integrity are easier to observe than to optimize.

We contribute:
(\textbf{C1})~a systematic empirical analysis of structural degeneration in GRPO-trained neurosymbolic reasoning, using LOO ablation (STA/ISR) to catalogue eight hacking modes across six reward conditions (\S\S\ref{sec:sta}--\ref{sec:crossdomain});
(\textbf{C2})~empirical characterization of a seed-dependent Goodhart effect when ISR is repurposed as reward, with matched controls ruling out confounds (\S\ref{sec:goodhart});
(\textbf{C3})~cross-logic replication (FOL/Prolog) confirming that the Goodhart effect transfers across logic types, with exploratory cross-dataset evidence (FOLIO/ProofFOL; \S\ref{sec:crossdomain}).

\label{sec:diagnostic}

\textbf{Collapse trajectory.}
Figure~\ref{fig:training-isr-curves} traces exec-only GRPO (seed~42) across 1000 training steps.
As PR climbs from $32\%$ to $96\%$, ISR$^*$ collapses from $0.642$ to $0.003$, a $214\times$ raw drop (partially attributable to premise-count inflation; matched comparison yields $1.06\times$, \S\ref{sec:goodhart}) consistent with structural degeneration behind apparent success. %% registry: plan003-eval-exec-only-r5, plan001-exec-only-s42-r2, plan016-extended-1000-s42
By 500 steps, $87\%$ of proved samples embed the conclusion verbatim (CE), establishing degeneration independent of the ISR scalar.
A binary variant testing only collective premise necessity misses the collapse entirely ($\text{ISR}{=}1.0$ for all proved samples), confirming that per-component LOO testing is essential (Appendix~\ref{app:o1-ablation}).
All three exec-only seeds converge to ${\leq}6.2\%$ non-hacking by 1000 steps (Table~\ref{tab:fol-detailed}), confirming that structural degeneration under exec-only reward is not seed-specific among converged runs.

\textbf{Cross-seed instability.}
Three seeds diverge into qualitatively different failure modes: conclusion embedding (s42), repetition collapse (s43), and tautological collapse (s44), yet all converge to ${\leq}7\%$ non-hacking by 1000 steps (Table~\ref{tab:fol-detailed}; Appendix~\ref{app:detailed-results}).
Exec-only seeds exhibit qualitatively different convergence patterns (PR range: 13--98\%), precluding meaningful pooling; we report seed~42 as representative of the converged regime (Table~\ref{tab:goodhart-landscape}). %% registry: plan001-exec-only-s42-r2, plan001-exec-only-s43-r4, plan001-exec-only-s44; plan016-extended-1000-s42, plan001-exec-only-s43-1000-r7, plan001-exec-only-s44-1000-r2
Two additional seeds under identical conditions fail to converge ($\text{PR}{\approx}14\%$; Appendix~\ref{app:seed-convergence}), retaining genuine reasoning and suggesting that structural degeneration requires successful convergence, though the confound between model capability and optimization success precludes strong causal attribution (Appendix~\ref{app:llama-coldstart}).

\textbf{SFT baseline.}
SFT on gold formalizations produces $92\%$ non-hacking outputs at $\text{PR}{=}13\%$ (odds ratio ${=}147$ vs.\ exec-only, $p{<}0.001$), establishing that hacking modes are absent before RL optimization.
SFT serves as a pre-RL reference rather than a PR-matched control; its low PR reflects minimal optimization pressure.
Early-training comparison supports this interpretation: at 100 steps ($\text{PR}{=}32\%$), hacking already reaches $31\%$ vs.\ SFT's $8\%$ at comparable PR (Appendix~\ref{app:100step}). %% registry: plan009-sft-baseline-s42-1000/s43-1000/s44-1000-r3

\subsection{Goodhart Effects on ISR-as-Reward}
\label{sec:goodhart}

We reverse ISR's role from observer to reward: $r = r_{\text{exec}}\cdot(1 + \beta\,\text{ISR})$, with matched random control ($u\sim\mathcal{U}(0,1)$).
The result is a regressional Goodhart pattern~\citep{manheim-goodhart-taxonomy}: optimizing a structural proxy shifts the \emph{composition} of failure modes without reducing their aggregate prevalence.
ISR reward systematically selects for tautological shortcuts (TS) over conclusion embedding (CE) rather than producing genuine reasoning (Table~\ref{tab:goodhart-landscape}). The scalar ISR$^*$ advantage ($214\times$ raw) collapses to $1.06\times$ after controlling for premise count; the compositional mode shift provides the primary evidence.

